# Which code is ours and which only travels with us.
#
# GitHub guesses a project's languages by counting file extensions, and it
# counts everything - including the four vendored libraries. zlib alone ships
# example bindings in Ada, C#, Pascal and Java under contrib, so the language
# bar announced this project as partly Ada. That is not a small cosmetic point:
# the bar is the first thing a visitor reads about what the code IS.
#
# linguist-vendored says "present, but not written here". The files still ship -
# they have to, the GPL asks for the complete source - they simply stop being
# counted as ours. THIRD-PARTY.md remains the place where their origin and
# licence are named; this file only fixes an arithmetic.
#
# The patterns start with **/ ON PURPOSE. Writing the full path would mean
# writing "TOMB5 Code/...", and a space separates a pattern from its attribute
# here - git then read "Code/TOMB5/..." as an attribute name and rejected the
# line. A leading **/ avoids the folder with the space entirely.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed: Preparation/Tools. Those PowerShell scripts and
# diagnostic programs are this project's own work, and the few per cent they
# contribute to the bar are honest.

**/tomb5/libs/zlib/**        linguist-vendored
**/tomb5/libs/stb/**         linguist-vendored
**/tomb5/libs/discordRPC/**  linguist-vendored
**/external/imgui/**         linguist-vendored
**/src/libs/stb/**           linguist-vendored

# Generated, not written: 780 KB of zlib-packed level zones and checksum
# lists as hex, produced by Preparation/Tools/EmbedData.ps1 (#142). Counted
# as C++ it would swamp the language bar the same way zlib's contrib did.
**/src/core/EmbeddedData.cpp linguist-generated

# The HD shaders check out with LINE FEEDS on every machine (audit round A1,
# #172). The engine has no runtime shader compiler: the bytecode travels as
# .inc byte arrays beside the .hlsl it came from, and every .inc carries the
# MD5 of that .hlsl on its last line so that be.bat can tell, without a build,
# whether the two still belong together (bshader.bat, :stamp).
#
# An MD5 is taken over BYTES, and line endings are bytes. On Windows git's own
# default, core.autocrlf=true, checks a text file out with carriage returns,
# while the file the stamp was taken over here had none - so the guard fired
# on a fresh clone, rebuilt shaders that were perfectly in step, and left the
# clone with two files modified that nobody had touched. It was worse than
# that: hd_room's stamp was the hash of an LF file and hd_model's the hash of
# a CRLF one, so whichever machine you stood on, one of the two pairs lied.
#
# eol=lf pins the checkout instead of teaching the batch file to normalise:
# one line of rule rather than a second hashing path in two scripts that would
# have to agree forever. These five files are C source and HLSL - no tool in
# this project cares which ending they carry, only that every machine sees the
# same one.
**/hd_shader/*.hlsl eol=lf
**/hd_shader/*.inc  eol=lf
